Ask the GL whether its fences work, rather than recognise its name.

Rule 190 takes GPU rasterization off the 2in1 emulator by matching Metal
in its GL version string. That catches the emulator we have, on the host
we have. The next emulator, or the same one on a Windows or Linux host,
translates through something else, reports a different name, and has the
same missing fence.

The emulator advertises EGL_KHR_fence_sync, so every check Chromium
already makes says fences are available -- GLFence::IsSupported reads the
extension string and nothing more. Then eglCreateSync fails and reports
EGL_SUCCESS as the error, which is the tell that nothing is implemented
behind the advertisement. GPU rasterization goes on to read tiles that
were never finished: stale tiles, tiles in the wrong place, black bands
down the bottom and the right, and a screenshot that comes back white.

So the call is made, once, while a context is current and just after the
lists have had their say -- late enough to overrule a list that said yes.
Rule 190 stays as a backstop for anything that fails earlier than this
runs.

